1
00:00:00,393 --> 00:00:03,168
Take one request about the order
validator.

2
00:00:03,243 --> 00:00:07,368
If you supply the function, Copilot can
explain its comparison.

3
00:00:07,443 --> 00:00:11,543
In an agent session, it may also choose
an available tool to find the file,

4
00:00:11,618 --> 00:00:14,031
read it, or validate a proposed change.

5
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:17,533
Open the training workspace and look at
the session.

6
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:21,003
Can you point to an actual tool event
and the information it returned?

7
00:00:22,664 --> 00:00:24,489
Watch the order of events.

8
00:00:24,539 --> 00:00:27,314
The agent reads that zero should be
rejected,

9
00:00:27,364 --> 00:00:31,061
then opens the validator and sees
greater than or equal to zero.

10
00:00:31,663 --> 00:00:35,688
That result gives it a reason to inspect
the tests for a zero case.

11
00:00:35,763 --> 00:00:38,763
Each next step depends on what the tool
returns.

12
00:00:38,838 --> 00:00:41,489
Check the event that led the agent to
its conclusion.

13
00:00:42,976 --> 00:00:45,551
Give Plan a task with a clear stopping
point.

14
00:00:45,626 --> 00:00:49,251
“Find why zero passes and describe one
regression test.

15
00:00:49,301 --> 00:00:52,034
Preserve the function signature and stop
after the plan.”

16
00:00:52,646 --> 00:00:55,921
Review the named files and the reason
for the test.

17
00:00:55,996 --> 00:00:59,571
If the plan adds a dependency or changes
other behavior,

18
00:00:59,646 --> 00:01:01,960
narrow it before moving on to
implementation.

19
00:01:03,456 --> 00:01:08,381
If the session asks to run tests, read
the command and the working directory.

20
00:01:08,431 --> 00:01:12,237
Our example uses Python’s unittest
discovery in the training checkout.

21
00:01:12,839 --> 00:01:14,914
That matches the agreed check.

22
00:01:14,989 --> 00:01:18,664
Verify where the process will actually
run and use the approved runner for this

23
00:01:18,714 --> 00:01:19,589
lab.

24
00:01:19,664 --> 00:01:23,364
If a command targets a different project
or publishes changes,

25
00:01:23,439 --> 00:01:25,332
stop and inspect what prompted it.

26
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:30,635
Our run reports that the zero test
failed because true was returned where

27
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:32,285
false was expected.

28
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:33,841
Find that line in the output.

29
00:01:34,455 --> 00:01:38,455
A chat message that says “tested” does
not tell you which case ran.

30
00:01:38,505 --> 00:01:41,680
The assertion helps you check the
proposed diagnosis.

31
00:01:41,755 --> 00:01:45,134
Keep the output with the revision so a
teammate can replay the result.

32
00:01:46,626 --> 00:01:51,171
In this workplace, Copilot in VS Code is
the local entry point.

33
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:55,323
Claude Code and OpenCode run in the
managed G C P workspace.

34
00:01:55,926 --> 00:01:59,476
Open the approved environment for the
tool you are using and confirm the

35
00:01:59,526 --> 00:02:00,901
working folder.

36
00:02:00,976 --> 00:02:04,088
A tool call uses the permissions and
runtime of its session.

37
00:02:04,697 --> 00:02:09,065
Check those before assuming a local file
or command is available remotely.

38
00:02:10,518 --> 00:02:13,018
Try a small read-and-report task.

39
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:16,168
Ask the agent to inspect the validator
and tests,

40
00:02:16,243 --> 00:02:20,718
identify the line causing the behavior,
and stop with the file path and relevant

41
00:02:20,768 --> 00:02:21,368
evidence.

42
00:02:21,992 --> 00:02:24,142
Review the actual steps it took.

43
00:02:24,217 --> 00:02:27,692
If it starts editing or proposes an
unrelated command,

44
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:30,346
pause and bring the request back to the
stated goal.
